<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Clement XIII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1693">1693</a>–<a href="/years/1769">1769</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clement XIII, born Carlo Rezzonico in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, was pope from 1758 to 1769. His pontificate was dominated by the defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> against the Catholic monarchies, which expelled them from Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and Parma. His Monitorium of 1768 against the church reforms of the Duchy of Parma led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> courts to occupy papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-clement-xiii.jpg" alt="Pope Clement XIII" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Raphael Mengs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anton_Raphael_Mengs_-_Portrait_of_pope_Clement_XIII,_c._1760.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carlo della Torre di Rezzonico was born on 7 March 1693 into a recently ennobled patrician family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. His father bought and finished the palace on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> now called Ca' Rezzonico. He received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, took a doctorate in canon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, and attended the Pontifical Academy of Ecclesiastical Nobles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. He became Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fano">Fano</a> in 1721, was ordained priest on 23 December 1731, and was made a cardinal by Pope Clement XII in 1737. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Padua from 1743 he visited his diocese often and reformed its running.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-xiv">Benedict XIV</a> died in 1758, negotiations between rival factions led to Rezzonico's election on 6 July 1758 with 31 votes of 44. He was crowned on 16 July. Modest in character and generous with his private fortune, he permitted vernacular translations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> in Catholic countries and placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a> of D'Alembert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Diderot</a> on the Index. He favoured dialogue with Protestants without compromise on doctrine, and recognised the Hanoverians as kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, refusing to recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a> as Charles III after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-francis-edward-stuart">James Francis Edward Stuart</a> in 1766.</p><p>His pontificate was dominated by pressure to suppress the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. In 1758 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a> expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> from Portugal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> suppressed them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by the arrêt of 2 August 1762, which Clement annulled, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> expelled them in November 1764. The bull Apostolicum pascendi of 7 January 1765 praised the order but was largely ignored. On the night of 2–3 April 1767 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> had every Jesuit house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> surrounded and the Jesuits shipped to Civitavecchia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1768 Clement issued a monitorium against the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-duke-of-parma">Philip, Duke of Parma</a>, and the question of Parma's investiture, technically a papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, worsened the dispute. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> kings seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> and Pontecorvo and in January 1769 demanded the total suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>. Clement agreed to call a consistory, but died on the eve of its meeting. Poison was suspected, but there appears to be no conclusive evidence.</p><p>He collapsed on the night of 2 February 1769 after a day of audiences and died quickly; an aneurysm near the heart is generally believed to have been the cause. He was buried in the Vatican on 8 February, and in 1774 his remains were moved to a monument sculpted by Antonio Canova.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Clement XIII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His ministers, above all du Tillot, pursued an enlightened programme that limited church privileges, and when <b>Pope Clement XIII</b> condemned these measures in the Monitorium of Parma of January 1768, the duchy responded by expelling the Jesuits.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1742" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Clement XIII"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1742">What was here in 1742? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
